WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
In the beginning of the 19th century,&nbsp;
most people in the western world had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.900
a solid theory about our origins: all&nbsp;
creatures were formed by a single creator.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:18.720
But then, in 1831, a 22-year old student&nbsp;
decided to go on an expedition that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.220
change everything. The young man&nbsp;
spent years on remote islands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.960
investigating living organisms and taking&nbsp;
careful notes on their differences.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:36.420
After close to five long years, the man&nbsp;
returned home. He organized his findings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.440
and started to write his own&nbsp;
theory on the origin of species.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:48.840
Decades later, in 1859, Charles Darwin,&nbsp;
supported by the work of Alfred Wallace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:55.080
challenged the status quo with his theory&nbsp;
of evolution - based on natural selection.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.360
Natural selection is the process through which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.160
populations of living organisms&nbsp;
adapt, reproduce, and change.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.120
This is possible because organisms&nbsp;
evolve when random mutations happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.760
in the DNA of reproductive cells.
Stored in cells the DNA contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:25.860
instructions that determine an organism's&nbsp;
characteristics early after conception.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:34.140
For instance, two animals with particular&nbsp;
features, can have a baby with very special ones.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.800
Since most of us are in competition&nbsp;
for limited resources, territory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.960
or potential partners, certain traits&nbsp;
are beneficial, while others are not.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.900
And when the survival of one is&nbsp;
challenged by the presence of another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:57.240
some can get by as they are, while&nbsp;
others must adapt or they will die out.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.760
One way to adapt is to specialize for particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.660
ecological niches in order&nbsp;
to survive and reproduce.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.420
Over time, it may be that there&nbsp;
are certain mutations that later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.660
happen to improve one's ability for reproduction.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.720
If these particular traits&nbsp;
are also heritable, that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.560
passed from parent to offspring,&nbsp;
then there will be a reproductive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:30.900
advantage - and that’s what modern&nbsp;
evolutionary theory defines as fitness.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:39.120
The phrase "survival of the fittest'' stands&nbsp;
for the process within a population, where fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:45.960
variants leave reproductive copies of themselves&nbsp;
and less fit variants reproduce less or disappear.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.320
Over generations, fit traits&nbsp;
become the dominant variation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.420
in a population and can eventually&nbsp;
emerge as a new species - a group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.560
similar organisms who interbreed&nbsp;
with each other - and no others.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:08.880
And as all of this happens in the surroundings&nbsp;
of the organism, which may change any time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:15.480
it’s their natural environment that "selects&nbsp;
for" how successful a species is at reproduction.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.380
And this is why the entire process&nbsp;
is known as natural selection.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:32.640
One famous real life example is the story&nbsp;
of peppered moths, which populated the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.140
United Kingdom with the perfect texture&nbsp;
to blend into their natural environment.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:45.960
In the 1800s, the industrial revolution&nbsp;
then changed that. Factories and homes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.920
darkened the skies and the smoke&nbsp;
from coal blackened everything.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:58.680
Over time the tree barks also became darker,&nbsp;
and white moths were more visible to predators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.980
while the less prevalent&nbsp;
black moths became invisible.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:07.260
As a result, peppered moths were more likely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:11.640
be eaten and dark ones ended up&nbsp;
having a reproductive advantage.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:18.900
By 1970, in some regions almost&nbsp;
all moths had dark wings.&nbsp;

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:24.420
They were not stronger, not faster,&nbsp;
not smarter, but simply fitter.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.820
As humans started paying&nbsp;
attention to the environment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:35.820
this tendency reversed. Today mostly&nbsp;
white moths dominate in the UK again.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.500
Random genetic mutations were giving&nbsp;
dark moths a reproductive advantage in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.320
a certain environment for&nbsp;
a certain period of time.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:52.740
What are your thoughts? Are&nbsp;
all species related? And if so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:57.480
do all stem from the same single&nbsp;
origin? And if that's the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:03.060
how do we solve the chicken and egg problem?&nbsp;
